WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:12.330
In June 2014, a single barrel of oil cost
$114.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:15.900
Today, its price is around $50.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:20.570
This drop is far beyond the usual fluctuations
in the price of this raw material and the

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:24.350
predictions of those pessimistic defenders
of the “peak oil” theory - the theory

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:33.440
that we are about to have a severe shortage
since petroleum is “about to run out”.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.050
The truth is that there are many different
factors which suggest that oil prices are

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.680
going to remain quite low for a long while.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.210
This is mainly due to two reasons:

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:47.800
The first is the constant improvement in energy
efficiency, meaning we have less need for

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:48.800
energy.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.860
It is logical when you think the OECD economies
- the group of wealthiest countries - use

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:58.739
now the same amount of primary energy as they
did in the year 2000 while their production

00:00:58.739 --> 00:01:00.829
has been a 30% higher.

00:01:00.829 --> 00:01:09.100
The 
second reason is innovation and technological

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:13.750
development - a field of expertise inherent
to human evolution.

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:18.470
A development which has happened on two fronts:
both in fossil fuels as well as renewable

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:19.470
energies.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:24.659
The best example of this might be “fracking”
- a technology that has provided the market

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:27.369
with large amounts of oil and natural gas.

00:01:27.369 --> 00:01:31.330
In fact, this technology has lead to the U.S.
almost doubling its levels of oil production.

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:37.400
The US is currently producing more old than
it has since the 1970s.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:41.340
The ecological impact of fracking has been
incredibly controversial, but that is a matter

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.409
for discussion in a future video.

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:47.829
The low price of oil has led to a terrible
setback for the countries in the Persian Gulf

00:01:47.829 --> 00:01:52.630
where 85% of their income relies on this resource.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:58.689
Up until now, oil has allowed the payment
of all kinds of bills.

00:01:58.689 --> 00:02:04.009
Citizens have got used to a high level of
subsidies and allowances, and not having to

00:02:04.009 --> 00:02:05.009
pay any taxes.

00:02:05.009 --> 00:02:09.840
Oil has subsidised water, electricity, fuels,
low interest loans…

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:20.099
Citizens have also got used to having nearly
the “right” to a well-paid job in the

00:02:20.099 --> 00:02:21.099
Civil Service.

00:02:21.099 --> 00:02:25.270
Furthermore, countries in the Persian Gulf
have spent enormous amounts of money on guns.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:32.810
They have spent so much money that Saudi Arabia
spends more money on defence than Russia (making

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:43.231
it the country with the third biggest military
budget in the world).

00:02:43.231 --> 00:02:46.150
Further, as we all know, oil has contributed
to the funding of pro-Islamic campaigns all

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:47.763
around the world and has paid, as well, for
the construction of mosques.

00:02:47.763 --> 00:02:53.640
However, these policies have become completely
unsustainable due to the drop in oil prices.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:03:07.120
&nbsp;In 2015 alone oil producing countries lost
$360bn in income as a result of the hard drop

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.950
of prices in this particular raw material.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.830
Now, they still do get a lot of money from
oil.

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:18.599
For example, Saudi Arabia gets a $5,500 income
per capita from their so-called “black gold”.

00:03:18.599 --> 00:03:24.000
But despite this high income they are unable
to maintain everything they have committed

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.000
to.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:28.880
In order to face this changing scenario, all
of the countries in the Persian Gulf are announcing

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:34.280
and developing ambitious plans to diversify
their economies and get some distance from

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:38.650
oil, following Dubai´s example in some ways
we because, as we saw in a previous video,

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:50.080
it is the only case of effective diversification
away from oil in the last 40 years.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:58.799
Because of this diversification the Emirates
is becoming a world leader in tourism, trade,

00:03:58.799 --> 00:04:06.459
and logistics.

00:04:06.459 --> 00:04:11.189
Tens of billions of dollars are being invested
into Qatar with the hope that it will &nbsp;become

00:04:11.189 --> 00:04:13.110
an economy of knowledge.

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:17.870
&nbsp;Proudly showing off their “Education City”
development - a huge academic complex where

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:23.410
some of the most prestigious schools can be
found, with many notably scientists working

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:26.360
with almost infinite research budgets.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:30.720
However, the most archetypical case is the
one we find in Saudi Arabia - one of the world’s

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.620
most suffocating and terrifying countries.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:37.500
Human Rights in Saudi Arabia are merely an
illusion.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:41.190
Women are forced to be marry underage and
they cannot drive.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:49.389
Dozens of executions can occur every year,
and torture is quite routine.

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:57.130
This country has even banned theatres and
nightclubs.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:03.770
In Saudi Arabia, religion and fundamentalism
are more important than anything else.

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:12.330
But their economic crisis cannot be escaped
and they are facing today a rather complex

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:13.680
situation…

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.650
In 2015, the deficit reached 15% of the GDP
with the situation continuing in 2016 and

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:24.380
threatening to swallow up every penny saved
during the “black gold” years in a very

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:25.380
short period of time.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:29.800
Further, unemployment is becoming a major
concern, especially among young people, where

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:34.380
the unemployment rate reaches the 40%.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:39.350
This is the reason why in April 2016, prince
Mohammed bin Salman, the strongest man in

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:44.860
the kingdom, announced an ambitious reform
plan known as “Saudi Vision 2030” the

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:59.350
goal of which is to transform the country's
economy completely.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:04.990
The plan aims to achieve effective independence
from oil by 2020, and transform Saudi Arabia

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:08.580
in one of the most globally competitive economies
by the year 2030.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:13.270
The first step for achieving this goal starts
by privatising part of ARAMCO, the world’s

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:19.680
biggest company, which controls 10% of global
oil production with an estimated value five

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.639
times higher than Apple.

00:06:21.639 --> 00:06:26.380
With all the money from that privatization,
addition of foreign reserves, other privitisations

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:31.639
of public companies, and its real state, Saudi
Arabia wants to build a sovereign wealth fund

00:06:31.639 --> 00:06:41.740
of $2 trillion so they can generate a more
predictable and stable income than that which

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:43.260
is currently coming from its oil.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:46.810
All told, they also want to eliminate most
of the subsidies that are threatening their

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:51.610
economy and invest in new sectors such as
mining, finance or, more importantly, their

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:59.800
military industry.

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:03.509
New taxes will also be introduced as an alternative
method of earning for the government.

00:07:03.509 --> 00:07:07.889
Furthermore, the Prince wants to change further
education, which is currently closer to the

00:07:07.889 --> 00:07:11.740
Quran, to something that more meets market
requirements.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:16.949
The big question is: will Saudi Arabia succeed?

00:07:16.949 --> 00:07:21.740
It is not an easy task for a country to quickly
become an industrial power and it doesn’t

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:25.820
seem likely that tourists will be willing
to visit a country where it is common to see

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:29.330
people stoned to death for adultery or homosexuality...

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:33.680
Having said all that, the world would benefit
from their success no matter how dreadful

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:35.980
we personally find their political system.

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:43.259
70% of the Saudi population is under 30 and
they will require two million jobs over the

00:07:43.259 --> 00:07:44.259
next decade.

00:07:44.259 --> 00:07:49.259
We all know that when frustration, unemployment,
and religious fundamentalism add up, the consequences

00:07:49.259 --> 00:07:51.889
are truly dangerous to us all.

00:07:51.889 --> 00:07:57.380
Dubai has proved that moderation and capitalism
can lead to changes in fundamentalist regimes.

00:07:57.380 --> 00:08:04.180
We can only hope that Saudi Arabia can successfully
follow the example set by Dubai.

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:06.620
So, what do you think?

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:11.069
Will Saudi Arabia succeed?

00:08:11.069 --> 00:08:18.419
Can capitalism encourage moderation in Saudi
Arabia and similar countries?

00:08:18.419 --> 00:08:22.070
Let us know what you think in the comments
below, and if you enjoyed the video, click

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:32.060
like and don’t forget to subscribe to our
channel by clicking the button on the screen

00:08:32.060 --> 00:08:33.520
now.

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:34.260
And I’ll see you in our next video.

